  "solution": {
    "text": "<blockquote><p>All the alkali earth metals, except beryllium combine with hydrogen upon heating to form their hydrides,<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#160;</mo><msub><mi>MH</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>BeH</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math>, however, can be prepared by the reaction of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>BeCl</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>LiAlH</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></math>. .</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>BeCl</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>LiAlH</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>&#8594;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>BeH</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>LiCl</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>AlCl</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math></p></blockquote>",
